// 0.0.4 T07 std library: `concurrent` module — channels.
//
// The functions in this module are compiler/VM primitives, not Fleen source
// (D8), so this file carries only documentation. `channel` is a reserved
// word (T07e constructor syntax) and `split` lives in the compiler's builtin
// table (typeck/builtins.rs), bound by the resolver from the module path.
//
//     import std.concurrent;
//
//     ch = channel<int>(2);            // buffered channel, capacity 2
//     ch = channel<int>(0);            // rendezvous (direct handoff)
//     peer = std.concurrent.split(ch); // second handle to the same buffer
//     std.async.run(producer(move ch), consumer(move peer));
//
// `channel<T>(cap)` is a reserved-word constructor expression: cap = 0 is a
// rendezvous channel (a `send` parks until a receiver takes the value
// directly), cap > 0 is buffered (a `send` parks only when full). Channels
// are non-Copy and move-only (T07d): `clone ch` is rejected, and `split` is
// the one explicit fork point — it returns a second independent handle to
// the same buffer. Values travel with `c.send(v)` / `c.recv()`, recognized
// in typeck (T07a): `send` suspends on a full buffer, `recv` on an empty
// one. There is no `close` in 0.0.4: a run ends when no task is runnable,
// and a program whose tasks are all blocked on channels is a
// `ChannelDeadlock` runtime error (T07b).
